WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.835
Effective detections focus on meaningful behaviors rather than isolated indicators.

00:00:04.835 --> 00:00:07.530
A single indicator may be useful in the right context,

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:12.055
but durable detections usually look for activity patterns that matter to defenders.

00:00:12.055 --> 00:00:15.480
Detection engineers think carefully about telemetry sources, field availability,

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:20.440
business context, investigation workflows, analyst usability, and operational impact.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.520
The best detection logic still fails if the data is unavailable or the alert is too vague

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.490
to investigate.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.680
Good detections answer a defensive question.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.020
They explain what was observed, why it may matter, which system or identity is involved,

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:35.980
what supporting context exists, and what the next investigative step should be.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:37.755
Signal quality matters more than alert volume.

00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:42.280
A detection program that produces thousands of low-confidence alerts can make the SOC

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.495
slower and less confident.

00:00:43.495 --> 00:00:46.880
A smaller set of well-designed detections can create more value.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.280
Building effective detections is therefore a design problem,

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.455
not just a query-writing task.

00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:54.740
Engineers balance sensitivity, specificity, context, maintainability,

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:58.600
and operational usefulness so defenders can make informed decisions.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.110
Context is especially important.

00:01:00.110 --> 00:01:03.885
Useful alerts often include related identity information, asset importance,

00:01:03.885 --> 00:01:06.785
recent activity, known ownership, enrichment results,

00:01:06.785 --> 00:01:09.970
and links to investigation guidance or case workflows.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:12.870
Effective detection design also considers failure modes.

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.430
Engineers ask what could create noise, what data delays may occur,

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:16.705
what fields may be missing,

00:01:16.705 --> 00:01:19.440
and how the detection should behave when the environment changes.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.040
Before deployment, detection engineers should also think about the analyst experience.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.010
A good alert should be understandable under pressure, during handoffs,

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.080
and by someone who did not write the detection.
